  "style": {
    "id": "caustics",
    "name": "Caustics",
    "description": "A sunlit room (white table, slate-blue wall): a clear, lumpy aqua glass slab in a slim steel frame rides a vertical brass rail above a white tabletop. A warm beam comes straight down through it. Close to the table the light is an even patch; as the slab rises the patch swirls into bright curved lines and dark gaps, and at exactly one height it unwinds into a white-hot picture on a dim ground while the room light falls. The light on the table is the glass's own ray mesh warped by its deflection field (light conserved per triangle: brightness = area on the glass / area on the table), so folds make real caustic lines, and the slab's bumps are shaded from the same field.",
